    "tilt": {
      "duration": 0.5,
      "loop": false,
      "note": "The second axis, and the second lookup table. Same 0.05 s per degree, over a -5..+5 range, so it is 0.5 s long. It keys `headroll`'s rotation, which `turn` also keys — which is the whole reason BOTH sliders declare `additive: true`: at the default a slider WRITES its animation's value outright, so whichever of the two sits later in the rig's constraints array would be the only one that moved the head (AUTHORING §3.5.2). Linear keys, for the reason `turn` gives.",

      "tracks": [
        {
          "bone": "headroll",
          "property": "rotate",
          "keys": [
            { "t": 0, "v": [-5] },
            { "t": 0.25, "v": [0] },
            { "t": 0.5, "v": [5] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "bone": "neck",
          "property": "rotate",
          "keys": [
            { "t": 0, "v": [-2] },
            { "t": 0.25, "v": [0] },
            { "t": 0.5, "v": [2] }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

    "sweep": {
      "duration": 3.2,
      "loop": true,
      "note": "⭐ The only animation here that is meant to be played, and it does not touch the face. It turns the two GAUGE NEEDLES, and the head follows because the sliders are reading them. All the shaping lives here — MOTION.md §3.6's anticipation is not in `turn` and cannot be, because `turn` is indexed by a value rather than by time. The two needles run on different waveforms so that a reader can see two independent numbers rather than one: yaw holds at each extreme, tilt does not.",

      "tracks": [
        {
          "bone": "yaw_dial",
          "property": "rotate",
          "keys": [
            { "t": 0, "v": [0], "ease": "swell" },
            { "t": 0.7, "v": [19], "ease": "settle" },
            { "t": 1.1, "v": [19], "ease": "swell" },
            { "t": 1.7, "v": [0], "ease": "swell" },
            { "t": 2.4, "v": [-19], "ease": "settle" },
            { "t": 2.8, "v": [-19], "ease": "swell" },
            { "t": 3.2, "v": [0] }
          ]
